## Enum Variants with Different Data

Each pet type needs different fields. An enum with data variants makes this clean:

### The Pet Enum

```xiom
enum Pet {
  Dog(breed: Str),
  Cat(color: Str),
  Fish(tank_size: Int),
  Bird(can_talk: Bool),
}
```

Each variant carries its own data. `Dog` holds a breed string; `Fish` holds a tank size integer.

### Creating Pets

```xiom
let buddy = Pet.Dog("Golden Retriever");
let whiskers = Pet.Cat("Orange");
let nemo = Pet.Fish(20);
let polly = Pet.Bird(true);
```

### Describing Any Pet

Use `match` to destructure and describe any pet:

```xiom
fn describe(p: Pet) -> Str {
  match p {
    Dog(breed) => { return "A " + breed + " dog"; }
    Cat(color) => { return "A " + color + " cat"; }
    Fish(tank_size) => { return "A fish needing " + tank_size + "L tank"; }
    Bird(can_talk) => {
      if can_talk {
        return "A talking bird";
      }
      return "A quiet bird";
    }
  }
}
```

### Pattern Matching in Action

```xiom
describe(buddy);    // "A Golden Retriever dog"
describe(whiskers); // "A Orange cat"
describe(nemo);     // "A fish needing 20L tank"
describe(polly);    // "A talking bird"
```

Each arm destructures the variant and gives you access to its data.

